<ServiceLayout
  title="Custom Concrete Driveways"
  eyebrow="Custom Concrete"
  intro="A driveway is the largest single piece of concrete on most properties and the first thing anyone sees. CPF pours stamped, colored and paver driveways across Thousand Oaks, Newbury Park and the wider Ventura County area."
  description="Custom concrete driveways in Thousand Oaks, Newbury Park and Ventura County: stamped concrete, colored and sealed finishes, paver driveways, sidewalk banding and integrated retaining walls. Family owned, owner supervised. CA License #878989, C-29 / C-8."
  headerAsset={A.beautifulStampedConcreteDriveway}
  serviceName="Custom concrete driveway construction"
  showcase={showcase}
  gallery={gallery}
  galleryHeading="Driveways, walkways and commercial concrete"
>
  <h2>The driveway is the first thing anyone sees</h2>
  <p>
    It is also the piece of concrete that takes the most abuse: vehicle loads,
    a full temperature swing every day, and whatever the ground underneath
    decides to do over twenty years. A driveway that cracks in its third summer
    was almost never a finishing problem. It was the base, the thickness, or
    the control joints.
  </p>

  <h3>Stamped and colored concrete</h3>
  <p>
    Stamping takes a poured slab and gives it the pattern and texture of stone,
    brick or tile at a fraction of the cost of laying the real material over
    that area. The result is only as good as the timing — stamps go in during a
    window measured in minutes, and a crew that misses it leaves the evidence in
    the surface permanently.
  </p>

  <h3>Paver driveways</h3>
  <p>
    Pavers buy you something concrete cannot: individual units that can be
    lifted and re-laid. If a utility has to come up, or a section settles, the
    repair is invisible. The tradeoff is the base preparation, which is more
    exacting than for a slab, not less.
  </p>

  <h3>Everything the driveway touches</h3>
  <p>
    Most of these jobs are not only a driveway. The grade has to be resolved
    where it meets the street, the garage slab and the front walk, and on a
    sloping lot that usually means a retaining wall in the same pour sequence.
    Doing it as one job is why the banding, the borders and the wall all line
    up — see the <a href={withBase('/stonework/')}>stonework</a> and
    <a href={withBase('/grading-and-excavation/')}>grading and excavation</a>
    pages for the parts of that work that happen before any concrete arrives.
  </p>

  <h2>Owner supervised, fully insured</h2>
  <p>
    A concrete pour takes the driveway out of use, and an open form is not
    something anyone wants sitting for a fortnight. <strong>Each project is owner
    supervised</strong> — we are the first in and the last out — and CPF is
    <strong>licensed, insured and bonded</strong> under C-29 masonry and C-8
    concrete.
  </p>
</ServiceLayout>
